`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:[e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://www.fmod.com/docs/2.04/studio/event-macro-controls-reference.html#stealing",children:"Studio stealing modes"})," distinguish Oldest/Furthest/Quietest (stop the selected instance), Virtualize (quiet instances continue without output and can become real as relative audibility or capacity changes) and None (refuse new playback while at the limit). These are event max-instance policies, not Core software-channel limits. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://www.fmod.com/docs/2.04/api/managing-resources-in-the-core-api.html",children:"Core resource management, Interaction with the Core API Virtual Voice System"})," documents that ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"EventInstance::isVirtual == false"})," can coexist with some or all underlying Core Channels being virtual because of the software-channel limit. A real Studio event therefore does not prove audible Core voices."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"Proposed acceptance contract."})," Record instance identity, API result, callback type, playback enum, pause state, event virtualization, applicable Core voice limits and stop intent separately. Join these observations to the existing captured-output evidence. Distinguish refusal, invalidated observation, normal native stop and verified audible completion. Neither STOPPED nor PLAYING nor event-level nonvirtual status is a standalone audible-success gate. Reconcile uncertain starts before retrying."]}),`
